WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
In this three-part video series,
I'll be doing a deep dive into iron.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:10.080
We'll look at the ferritin blood test,
what that tells us about iron stores,

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.200
and why vegetarians
tend to hit the sweet spot

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.280
since higher levels are
associated with chronic disease

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:18.640
like type 2 diabetes and cancer.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.400
That's why you can
decrease cancer rates

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.840
when you bleed people
through blood donations.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.440
What's the healthiest way to
maintain optimal iron levels?

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:29.120
Let's dig in.

00:00:29.422 --> 00:00:35.729
"Are the Lower Iron Stores in
Vegetarians a Risk or a Benefit?"

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.720
The ferritin blood test
measures iron stores,

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.880
and a typical normal value
is in the 30 to 300 range.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.880
Almost universally,

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.360
vegetarians have
lower ferritin levels

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:50.960
than nonvegetarians;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.760
for example, 39 versus 77.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.760
In one of the largest studies
ever done,

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.040
using that 30 cut-off
as a sign of iron deficiency,

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.600
about half
of the menstruating vegetarians

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:03.440
scored as iron deficient,

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.040
compared to only about a third
of the menstruating omnivores.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.720
But lower iron stores
may actually be better,

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.880
shooting for scores
between 15,

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:14.960
which is the World Health
Organization's cut-off

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:16.160
for iron deficiency,

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:17.600
and 50,

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:22.400
because levels above 50
are associated with disease.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:23.200
In that case,

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.240
it could be vegetarians
are in the sweet spot,

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.120
because iron
is a double-edged sword,

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:30.800
producing free radicals
in the body

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:32.560
that can cause
oxidative damage

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:36.000
to our proteins
and very DNA.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.000
For example,
high iron intake

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.160
is a risk factor
for type 2 diabetes,

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:41.840
contributing to all
the cardinal features

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:42.880
of the disease,

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:46.320
and that's true across
the normal range of iron levels,

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:49.280
not just for people with some
kind of iron overload disease.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:51.520
So, the "elevated" ferritin,

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.840
that's a risk factor
for diabetes,

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:57.200
can just be the average
typical ferritin level,

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.800
increasing the odds of diabetes
by 20% compared to low levels,

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.880
whereas high ferritin
is even worse,

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.760
increasing the odds by 43%.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:06.880
Wait a second, though.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:09.680
Might higher iron stores
just be a proxy

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:11.600
for more meat consumption?

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:12.960
After all,
based on studies

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.280
encompassing
nearly 2 million people,

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:17.040
higher consumption
of all types of meat—

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.600
red meat, poultry,
processed, unprocessed—

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:22.960
is associated with
higher rates of diabetes.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:25.920
Why do we think it's the iron
and not just the saturated fat

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.320
and all the other harmful stuff
in meat?

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:32.320
Because genetic evidence
supports a causal link

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.040
between increased
systemic iron status

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:36.560
and increased diabetes risk.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.560
In other words,
regardless of what you eat,

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.640
if you just happened
to be born with genes

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:43.280
that tend to give you
higher iron stores,

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:46.560
you tend to have
more diabetes risk.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:50.480
Ideally, we'd prove it
with an interventional trial.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:51.680
Check it out.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.480
Researchers looked
at lean, healthy men and women

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.800
and found out
that the vegetarians

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:00.196
were more insulin sensitive
than the meat eaters.

00:03:00.196 --> 00:03:02.160
Prediabetes and type 2 diabetes

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:05.360
are diseases
of impaired insulin sensitivity.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.680
So, the vegetarians
were doing better.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.560
The vegetarians also had
lower ferritin levels.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.800
To test whether
their iron status

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.560
might explain
their advantage,

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:16.640
iron levels
in six male meat eaters

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:19.040
were lowered by phlebotomy—

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.120
bloodletting,
like donating blood—

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.320
to levels similar
to those seen in vegetarians.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:27.120
So, they were bled
so their ferritin dropped

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.080
from over 70
down closer to 30.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:31.680
And what happened?

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:35.920
Their insulin sensitivity
improved by about 40%.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:38.480
The iron-lowering trial
using blood draws

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:41.120
demonstrated that iron
is indeed a key factor

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.120
in the difference
in insulin resistance

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:45.840
among vegetarians
and meat eaters.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.000
Iron can also be
a double-edged sword

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.600
when it comes to cancer.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.400
Cancer can be viewed
as a ferrotoxic disease,

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.400
a disease of iron toxicity,

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:55.760
as iron plays
an important role

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:57.040
in oxidative tissue damage

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:58.960
and subsequent
cancer development.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:02.960
But again, a link between
high iron stores and cancer

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:04.000
could just be a link

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.320
between meat consumption
and cancer.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:09.040
Too bad we couldn't do some
kind of blood donation study

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:12.880
with cancer; oh, but we can.

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:16.720
Decreased cancer risk
after iron reduction.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:19.360
More than a thousand
cancer-free individuals

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:22.400
randomized to give
blood donations or not

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:25.040
were followed for the next
four and a half years,

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:29.200
and those who gave blood
had a 60% lower risk

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:31.520
of dying from cancer.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:34.320
Wait! They cut their risk
of dying from cancer

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:37.840
by more than half
just because they donated blood?

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:40.240
The accompanying editorial
remarked that those results

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:42.800
seemed almost too good
to be true!

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:44.080
Reducing one's iron stores

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:46.640
appeared to rapidly decrease
cancer development,

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:50.400
suggesting that just regular
ambient levels of iron stores

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.760
may be noxious and constitute
a public health threat.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:57.520
So, there may be a need
to redefine the normal range

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:58.960
for blood ferritin.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:01.680
It should be normal
based on what's healthy,

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:04.320
not just based on
what happens to be common.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:07.760
Yes, when ferritin slips
to less than like 12 ng/mL,

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:10.080
that is too low,

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.120
but iron toxicity diseases
may start with ferritin levels

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:14.880
greater than about 50 ng/mL.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.920
So, yes, vegetarians are more
likely to have lower iron stores

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:21.600
compared to non-vegetarians.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:23.600
However, since high iron stores

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:26.000
are also a risk factor
for certain chronic diseases,

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:27.920
such as type 2 diabetes,

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:30.320
it's recommended
that we all need to make sure

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:31.520
we're not getting too much,

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:34.080
and we can do that
by improving our diet

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:39.023
by consuming more plants
and less meat.

